Here's the full picture:

## What happened

In .NET 11, **DSA was removed on macOS**. `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS. This is because Apple's security frameworks dropped DSA, and .NET 11 followed suit. DSA still works on Windows and Linux.

## How to fix it

Since you need to **verify legacy DSA signatures**, you can't just switch algorithms — you're stuck with existing signatures. Your options:

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| **Bouncy Castle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle`) | Pure managed DSA, works everywhere | Extra dependency |
| **Platform guard** | No new deps | No macOS support at all |
| **Offload to Linux/Windows service** | Uses built-in APIs | Architecture change |

**Recommended fix** — use Bouncy Castle for macOS, built-in for others:

```csharp
// Add: dotnet add package Portable.BouncyCastle
bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] data, byte[] signature, DSAParameters keyParams)
{
    if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    {
        // Use Bouncy Castle on macOS
        var dsaParams = new Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters.DsaPublicKeyParameters(
            new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, keyParams.Y),
            new Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters.DsaParameters(
                new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, keyParams.P),
                new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, keyParams.Q),
                new Org.BouncyCastle.Math.BigInteger(1, keyParams.G)));
        var signer = Org.BouncyCastle.Security.SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
        signer.Init(false, dsaParams);
        signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
        return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
    }
    else
    {
        using var dsa = DSA.Create();
        dsa.ImportParameters(keyParams);
        return dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
    }
}
```

Long-term, plan to migrate away from DSA entirely — it's considered legacy on all platforms.
